MLLM description: Rapeseed plants are characterized by their tall, slender stems that can grow up to 6 feet in height. They have small, delicate yellow flowers that bloom in dense clusters along the stem. The leaves are narrow and elongated, with a slightly serrated edge. The flowers are typically bright yellow, with four petals each, and are arranged in a loose, open cluster at the top of the stem.

The color pattern is predominantly yellow, with the flowers being the most vividly colored part of the plant. The leaves are a lighter green, providing contrast against the yellow blooms. The size and proportions of the plant are relatively consistent across different varieties, with the flowers being the smallest part of the plant and the leaves being the largest.

Rapeseed plants do not exhibit any specific behavioral characteristics as they are stationary and rooted in the ground. However, they are known to be highly sensitive to environmental factors such as temperature, soil quality, and water availability.

The unique identifying traits of rapeseed include its tall stature, yellow flowers, and narrow, serrated leaves. These features set it apart from other similar plants, such as mustard or canola, which may have slightly different flower colors or leaf shapes.
